Keep the Metal buffer manager's free list at most 64 MiB (Minato, patch 0029).

Why: in the background no command buffer completes (patch 0025 keeps them for the foreground), so freeUnusedBuffers
moves nothing and every buffer an app makes there (glBufferData) is a new one. Back in the foreground they complete
and all come to the free list at once, and collectGarbage keeps them until 120 context switches, 5000 commits or 64 MiB
more allocated. A Ren'Py game rebuilds its display in the background: its free list held about 1 GiB after two returns
in the Simulator (where Metal's buffers are shared memory), and it never shrank. Android's GPU runs in the background,
so its buffers are reused there and none pile up.
Now the free lists count their bytes, and when a move to them leaves more than kMaxFreeBytes (64 MiB), the largest
free buffers go first until they fit; the small ones a frame makes again and again stay for reuse.
Test: graphics/tests/angle_buffer_pool.mm (20 buffers of 8 MiB drawn from and deleted in the background, three
returns: the free list holds at most 64 MiB after each, and small buffers are still reused).
Upstream: not planned (only an embedder that defers commits fills the free list this way).
